### Language design decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|:---------|:----------|
| Bengali `ধরো` for "let" | Common Bengali imperative meaning "hold/assume", natural for variable declaration |
| Bengali `ছাপাও` for "print" | Direct Bengali word for "print/stamp" |
| Bengali `যদি` / `নাহলে` for if/else | Standard Bengali conditionals |
| Bengali `যতক্ষণ` for "while" | Means "as long as" — natural loop construct |
| Marathi `घ्या` for "let" | Means "take" — imperative form, distinct from Hindi `मानो` |
| Marathi `लिहा` for "print" | Means "write" — distinct from Hindi `लिखो` |
| Marathi `जर` / `नाहीतर` for if/else | Standard Marathi conditionals |
| Marathi `जोपर्यंत` for "while" | Means "until" — standard Marathi, distinct from Hindi `जबतक` |
| Nepali `मान` for "let" | Means "value/consider" — distinct from Hindi `मानो` (by omitting the vowel ending) |
| Nepali `छाप` for "print" | Means "stamp/print" — distinct from Hindi `छापो` |
| Nepali `यदि_हो` for "if" | Means "if it is" — distinct from Hindi `यदि` (added `_हो`) |
| Nepali `जबसम्म` for "while" | Means "until" — standard Nepali |
| All three use `_` compound words | For multi-word concepts like `else_if`, to avoid whitespace issues in the regex-based transpiler |

